<a name="step_104-Intro"></a>
  <h1>Introduction</h1>

  This tutorial implements a matrix free algorithm
  that works on GPUs using
  Kokkos to solve the Stokes problem

  @f{align}{
    -\Delta \mathbf{u} + \nabla p &= \mathbf{f} \quad \text{in } \Omega, \\
    \nabla \cdot \mathbf{u} &= 0 \quad \text{in } \Omega, \\
    \mathbf{u} &= \mathbf{0} \quad \text{on } \partial\Omega,
  @f}

  where we want to solve for velocity $\mathbf{u}$ and pressure $p$.
  The Stokes equations model the behavior of slow-moving, viscous flow,
  which is often relevant in applications of biofluid dynamics, microfluidics,
  and flow of geomaterials like ice or rock.

  Finite element simulations on GPUs offer several benefits compared to traditional CPU calculations,
  for example a higher compute performance and memory bandwidth
  for a specified power budget as discussed in step-64.
  Just as in that tutorial, which solves the Helmholtz equation instead of the Stokes equations,
  we again make use of the Kokkos library to implement a portable algorithm that works well on
  both GPUs and CPUs.

  Unlike step-64, this tutorial solves for two solution variables, namely velocity $\mathbf{u}$ and
  pressure $p$, which means we will need two DoFHandlers and Portable::FEEvaluation
  objects, and the solution will be stored in a block vector with two blocks.

  The algorithm that is implemented in this tutorial is matrix-free, meaning we use no assembled matrices.
  Matrix-free methods have been instrumental in speeding up finite element
  computations, as memory bandwidth is often a massive bottleneck when using
  matrix-based methods. Matrix-free methods exploit the fact that simple
  computations such as addition and multiplication are relatively quick
  compared to memory bandwidth, so instead of storing data in a matrix and
  using them to apply the action of the linear operator,
  matrix vector products are computed on the fly. This approach
  is discussed in detail in step-37.

  Shouldnt the paragraph above also contain references to the deal.II classes that make all this possible? Above you mention FEEvalution, but shouldnt you also mention PortableFEEvalution, and Portable::MatrixFree (or some relevant namespace or types)? step-64 already explains this, so maybe it is enough to add a reference to it, but at the moment it does not become clear from the text why all of this works on a GPU (it could describe a normal CPU based matrix-free algorithm). Also the MGTwoLevelTransferCopyToHost class should be mentioned and explained somewhere.

  While this program runs (slowly) without a GPU, using a workstation with one or more GPUs or a GPU cluster
  is strongly recommended. Unfortunately, installing deal.II with GPU support is non-trivial. Please see the
  [deal.II GPU wiki page](https://github.com/dealii/dealii/wiki/GPU-support-in-deal.II) for installation
  instructions.

  <h3>Weak form</h3>

  Multiplying the first equation by $\mathbf{v} \in H_0^1 = \{t \in H^1(\Omega)^d :
  t = 0 \text{ on } \partial\Omega\}$ and integrating by parts we get

  @f{eqnarray*}{
    (\nabla \mathbf{u}, \nabla \mathbf{v})_{\Omega} - (p, \nabla \cdot \mathbf{v})_{\Omega}
    = (\mathbf{f}, \mathbf{v})_{\Omega}.
  @f}

  Multiplying the second equation by $q \in L^2(\Omega)$ we get

  @f{eqnarray*}{
    (q, \nabla \cdot \mathbf{u})_{\Omega} = 0.
  @f}

  In order to perform numerical computations, we discretize the weak form and
  choose finite-dimensional finite element spaces to work with. We want to
  choose a pair of discrete velocity and pressure spaces that
  guarantees the existence of a unique computed solution. In this tutorial, we
  use $Q_{\text{degree}+1}^d$ for velocity and $Q_{\text{degree}}$
  for pressure, where degree $\geq 1$ and $d$ is the number of spatial
  dimensions. These pairs are known as Taylor-Hood elements, which satisfy the
  LBB condition and therefore guarantee existence and uniqueness of a
  numerical solution. Our code implements the $Q_2^d \times Q_1$ pair in
  particular. Of course, the Taylor-Hood pair can be changed by modifying
  <code>degree_p</code> in the code, which also changes the velocity degree
  which is set to <code>degree_p + 1</code>. This is also discussed in
  step-22, though that tutorial differs slightly in that a deformation tensor
  is used in place of $\nabla \mathbf{u}$. I was a bit puzzled about the purpose of this paragraph, because it is all already explained in step-22 and not directly related to the teaching points of this step. Step-31 and 32 also just refer to 22 when explaining their finite elements. I think the tutorial would be easier to read if you state that you generally follow the discretization of step-22, and then focus on what is different from 22 (i.e. everything related to matrix-free on GPUs).
Oh, and if you want to keep this paragraph, because it is important to the change of this step, you would need to spell this out. In this case you also need to at mention what the LBB conditions are, for users who havent stumbled across them in the other steps.

  <h3>Linear system and preconditioning</h3>

  The discretized linear system is of the form

  @f{eqnarray*}{
    Mx :=
    \begin{bmatrix} A & B^T \\ B & 0 \end{bmatrix}
    \begin{pmatrix} \mathbf{u} \\ p \end{pmatrix}
    =
    \begin{pmatrix} f \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}.
  @f}

  The eigenvalues of $M$ are not clustered favorably.
  Here it would be helpful to define what A, B, and B^T are. Also this statement comes a bit out of the blue, do we discuss the eigenvalues and preconditioner choices already in step-22 or step-31? Then maybe refer there for a discussion?
  Consequently, an iterative
  solver would not converge in a reasonable number of iterations. To address this
  issue, we use a block triangular right preconditioner of the form

  @f{align}{
    P^{-1}
    &= \begin{bmatrix} \widetilde{A} & B^T \\ 0 & -\widetilde{S}
       \end{bmatrix}^{-1} \\
    &= \begin{bmatrix} \widetilde{A}^{-1} & \widetilde{A}^{-1} B^T
      \widetilde{S}^{-1} \\ 0 & -\widetilde{S}^{-1} \end{bmatrix},
  @f}

  where $\widetilde{A} \approx A$ and $\widetilde{S} \approx S =
  BA^{-1}B^T$, which is known as the Schur complement. Note that some texts
  define the Schur complement to be $S = -BA^{-1}B^T$, but in this
  tutorial as well as step-32 we define $S$ to be a positive operator.

  Notice that

  @f{eqnarray*}{
    \begin{bmatrix} A & B^T \\ B & 0 \end{bmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix} \widetilde{A}^{-1} & \widetilde{A}^{-1} B^T \widetilde{S}^{-1} \\
    0 & -\widetilde{S}^{-1} \end{bmatrix}
    =
    \begin{bmatrix} A\widetilde{A}^{-1} &
    (A\widetilde{A}^{-1})B^T\widetilde{S}^{-1} - B^T\widetilde{S}^{-1} \\
    B\widetilde{A}^{-1} & B\widetilde{A}^{-1}B^T\widetilde{S}^{-1}
    \end{bmatrix}.
  @f}

  We see that if $\widetilde{A}^{-1} = A^{-1}$ and
  $\widetilde{S}^{-1} = (BA^{-1}B^T)^{-1}$ this is equal to

  @f{eqnarray*}{
    \begin{bmatrix} I & 0 \\ BA^{-1} & I \end{bmatrix}.
  @f}

  In other words, with perfect approximations of the inverse of $A$ and
  inverse of $BA^{-1}B^T$, the preconditioned system only has $1$ as
  an eigenvalue and therefore GMRES should converge in at most two iterations.

  Of course, it is more computationally efficient to choose
  $\widetilde{A}^{-1}$ and $\widetilde{S}^{-1}$ to be rough
  approximations of $A^{-1}$ and $(BA^{-1}B^T)^{-1}$.
  This "of course" is not obvious to a tutorial reader. Maybe replace with something like: "Of course computing A^-1 and S^-1 exactly is only slightly cheaper than computing the inverse of the whole block system, and way too expensive to create a (good) preconditioner. Instead it is more computationally efficient to ...
  In
  particular, we approximate $A$ with a single V-cycle application of a geometric multigrid (GMG)
  preconditioner and $S$ with the
  inverse pressure mass matrix $M_p$. This approximation of the Schur
  complement is standard in earth modeling and is implemented in the ASPECT
  geodynamics code. These approximations have been shown to be highly
  effective for problems featuring viscosity contrasts up to 6
  orders of magnitude.

  Letting $y = Px$, we can use an iterative solver to find $y$
  and deduce that $x = P^{-1}y$. I dont see where this fits into the rest of the tutoral, you dont use y or P above as far as I can see.

  For the actual preconditioner implementation, we require matrix-free
  operators for the Stokes system, the $A$ block, the Schur complement
  $S = BA^{-1}B^T$, and $B^T$.


  <h3>GPU Acceleration</h3>

  Not all parts of this example run on the GPU. Partly, this is because
  GPU support in deal.II is still early and some parts could and most certainly
  will be ported to run on GPUs. Additionally, some steps in this program
  do not lend themselves to GPU acceleration because they are hard to (or impossible)
  to parallelize and because they require IO, complex logic, or dynamic memory allocation.

  As in step-64, we use the template parameter
  MemorySpace::Default and MemorySpace::Host to indicate where the solution data
  of a particular vector is stored. MemorySpace::Host always indicates the host
  memory (CPU or main memory), while MemorySpace::Default refers to the memory of
  the default compute device (either CPUs or GPUs depending on how the Kokkos library
  used by deal.II was configured). While LinearAlgebra::distributed::Vector has
  a default value for the memory space, which is, confusingly, MemorySpace::Host,
  we always specify the memory space explicitly in this example. Any function operating
  on vectors stored on the compute device have to run on the GPU as a kernel and
  has to be marked with the DEAL_II_HOST_DEVICE precompiler macro to let the compiler
  know that this code has to be compiled for the programming model of the relevant
  compute device. This implies that the code in these functions cannot make use of
  functionality that is not available on this device. Before the CPU can operate on
  a vector, the data has to be copied into system memory (by copying into a vector with
  MemorySpace::Host).

  In short, we currently perform setup and postprocessing on the CPU, while the
  linear solve runs on the GPU. The example performs the following steps:

  1. Setup
    1.1. Generate and refine the mesh (MPI parallel, CPU)
    1.2. Distribute DoFs, compute constraints (CPU)
    1.3. Assemble right-hand side vector (CPU)
    1.3. Create mesh hierarchy for multigrid (CPU)
    1.4. Setup Portable::MatrixFree operator: evaluate inverse Jacobians, mapping, cell geometry (CPU)
    1.5. Setup multigrid operators, smoothers, transfer (CPU)
  2. Solve
    2.1. GMRES algorithm (CPU logic, operations on GPU):
         - Vector operations: inner products, ... (GPU)
         - Matrix-Vector product: Portable::MatrixFree (GPU)
         - GMG preconditioner: Smoother, transfer, coarse solve (GPU)
  3. Postprocess
    3.1. Move solution to host memory
    3.2. Compute errors (currently CPU only)

  Notice that roughly speaking the setup and postprocess happens on the CPU with the linear solve step happening on the GPU. With
  complex problems, this will be the majority of the computational time.

  @note The GMG transfer currently uses MGTwoLevelTransferCopyToHost, which, as the name implies, performs the operation
  on the CPU as the GPU implementation has not been completed.

  <h3>Test case</h3>

  Our test problem features a unit hypercube with the manufactured solution

  @f{align}{
    u &= \pi \sin^2(\pi x) \sin(2\pi y), \\
    v &= -\pi \sin(2\pi x) \sin^2(\pi y), \\
    p &= \cos(\pi x) \cos(\pi y)
  @f}

  in two dimensions and

   @f{align}{
   u &= \pi  \sin^2(\pi x)(\sin^2(\pi z)\sin(2\pi y)-\sin^2(\pi y)\sin(2\pi
   z)), \\
   v &= \pi  \sin^2(\pi y)  (\sin^2(\pi x)  \sin(2\pi z) - \sin^2(\pi
   z)\sin(2\pi x)), \\
   w &= \pi \sin^2(\pi z)  (\sin^2(\pi y)  \sin(2\pi x) -
   \sin^2(\pi x)\sin(2\pi y)), \\
   p &= \cos(\pi x)\cos(\pi y)\cos(\pi z)
   @f}

  in three dimensions.

  Since in the code you also have to specify the right-hand side, it would make sense to define it here as well, to have a full description of the problem in the text, including boundary conditions.
